<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malcolm II of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 954–<a href="/years/1034">1034</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Máel Coluim mac Cináeda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malcolm II was King of Scots from 1005 to 1034. His victory over the Northumbrians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carham">Battle of Carham</a> around 1018 secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a> and the Tweed frontier, and the British kingdom of Strathclyde came under Scottish control during his reign. He had no sons, and the throne passed to his grandson Duncan I, founding the line of kings that ruled medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Máel Coluim mac Cinaeda, anglicised Malcolm II, was a son of King Kenneth II and a grandson of Malcolm I. The Prophecy of Berchán says his mother was a woman of Leinster. Succession among Scottish kings of the time often involved murder, and Malcolm killed Kenneth III in 1005 in a battle at Monzievaird in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strathearn">Strathearn</a>. The Irish annals call him ard rí Alban, high king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, though the geographical area of modern Scotland also held a king of Strathclyde in the south-west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norse-gaels">Norse–Gaels</a> kings on the western coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a>, and the kings or mormaers of Moray, his nearest and most dangerous rivals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The first reliable report of the reign is an attack in 1006 on the lands of the Northumbrian rulers of Bamburgh, perhaps the customary raid of a new king, which included a siege of Durham. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> it ended in a heavy defeat by Uhtred of Bamburgh. A second war, probably in 1018, went better. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carham">Battle of Carham</a>, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tweed">River Tweed</a>, the Scots under Malcolm and the men of Strathclyde under their king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-the-bald">Owain Foel</a>, were victorious. A twelfth-century tract claims that Eadwulf Cudel, Uhtred's brother, then surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a> to Malcolm.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> reports that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> led an army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> on his return from a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It dates this to 1031, though there are reasons to place it in 1027. The Burgundian chronicler Rodulfus Glaber describes Malcolm as powerful in resources and arms and very Christian, and says the two made peace. Cnut came away with a promise of peace and friendship, and the Chronicle remarks that Malcolm kept to the submission only a little while.</p><p>Malcolm had no surviving sons. He arranged marriages for his daughters with the regional dynasties that might otherwise have rivalled him. Bethóc married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crinan-of-dunkeld">Crínán of Dunkeld</a>, and a daughter named Donada is said by later writers to have married Finlay of Moray, father of Macbeth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Marianus Scotus gives the date of Malcolm's death as 25 November 1034, and the king lists place it at Glamis Castle. The Annals of Tigernach call him the honour of all the west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, while later accounts say he was killed fighting bandits. His grandson Duncan I succeeded him as his chosen heir and reigned for five years and nine months. Tradition from the time of John of Fordun, if not earlier, called a Pictish stone at Glamis King Malcolm's grave stone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malcolm II of Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Malcolm II</b>’s victory at the Battle of Carham, around 1018, secured Lothian between the Forth and the Tweed, and the British kingdom of Strathclyde was absorbed during the eleventh century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Glamis, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.61,-3&amp;year=1006" aria-label="Glamis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Malcolm II of Scotland"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4859 5287h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4859 5287h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=56.61,-3&amp;year=1006">What was here in 1006? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
